  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-y)\\le x\\le 1}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y+1)\\le x\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y+1)\\,\u27f9\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-y)\\,\u27f9\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
